speak to mark dow a professor of  communications at the american  university of beirut thanks for joining  us mark  at the end of the day i spoke to zaina  about this the lebanese people want  accountability and justice and are they  likely ever to get that now given what's  unfolded on the streets this morning  uh it's obvious that that is a very  difficult road eventually they will get  there but what's standing in front of  them and facing them off  is basically militias militias with  weapons who  attempt to undermine government by  threatening to leave government  whose security agency officers  visit the courthouse and issue threats  to judges and then leak them to the  press and third they mobilize people in  the streets with weapons with  rocket launchers and automatic machine  guns and fire at homes and instigate  fights within the peaceful streets of  beirut all to prevent  ministers who have been accused and  already indicted in corruption through  international sanctions that have been  imposed on them all of this six people  died today several tens of people  injured a lot of destruction all because  two corrupt ministers  do not want to attend a hearing  with the judge who has accused them of  negligence for their performance when  they were ministers  and when those ammonium nitrates that  exploded in lebanon the third biggest  non-nuclear explosion in the history of  mankind those people don't want to even  show up to the investigation they are  both  now  uh uh  running away from justice they are uh  out there and they're trying to protect  themselves with armed protesters  unfortunately  it has caused six people to die because  two corrupt politicians don't want to  show up so yes justice is on the right  course the illegal attempts at  undermining and removing the judge have  both times failed the third one is going  to be through political pressure and  thus they have went through  terrorizing the streets and increasing  pressure on the government that they  have and there was  leaks from the minutes of the meeting of  the ministers and the minister of  culture who is a hezbollah and partisan  he basically said you either take a  political and legal decision to remove  the judge which is an intervention by  the executive power into the judicial  power or we will no longer allow this  government to meet  and he threatened the entire government  including the president of the country  the prime minister and everyone else and  that is where it stopped political  pressure and now the government is in  limbo this government that is supposed  to be doing something to alleviate the  economic situation and the hardships in  lebanon just got itself again  held hostage by hezbollah their arms  their militias in the street in politics  because they participate in the  government only to protect the interests  of iran and their militias and their  interventions in syria and other places  justice in lebanon is not a priority for  them it is always a conspiracy theory to  try and undermine them on twitter and  other social media their partisans today  are saying that this judge just became  the beach head for the u.s intervention  into lebanese politics another lie  creating another fake conspiracy theory  to try and undermine justice the rule of  law the government and the entire state  of lebanon there should be a firm  response from the government there  should be a call for a meeting  immediately for the government and it  should take steps to protect judge  justice and the independence of the  judiciary the judge today got the right  ruling which is the ruling that he can  continue to perform his duties  against the  attempt of the ministers to remove him  he is back on his job tomorrow morning  and they should show up to the court of  justice and he has every right to arrest  them  mark given the political situ this the  political system rather what does the  future hold for lebanon how do we  escape the cycle of so-called corruption  uh  going forward  i believe that process is about a  complete reform uh we cannot reform this  country with the same people who got us  into this mess we cannot reform the  country with  militias  independently from the state operating  their own foreign policy opening their  own wars and then dictating what happens  in terms of the operation of the  security the judiciary and others so  this requires a formal reform and this  is a demand on the president the prime  minister and everyone who is in the  state as well as the opposition to this  governed government that they need to  stand to protect the state from the  militias  hezbollah is the main organization  undermining the state the only way that  will happen it will not happen by any  international intervention there are  deals happening all across the region  between the french the americans are  withdrawing from the region so it's down  to lebanese local political parties to  stand and say we want to run this  country  based on the constitution and the laws  no longer by force of weapons therefore  what is needed now is reform and  reformists who are supporting the  functioning of the state the explosion  the investigation is a real test if that  logic can rule or again lebanon falls  into the trap of being ruled by a gun by  a militia for the purpose of serving  iran's interest in lebanon  mark certainly reform is an uphill  challenge for the country and you're  saying that it needs to be lebanese-led  but surely the international community  can put pressure on the government what  would you be hoping or what do you think  the international international  community is viewing or how do you think  they're viewing what's unfolded today  i believe they realized that it is not  enough to do economic reform when they  say reforms it has to be political  reforms and pushing forward withholding  parliamentary elections and municipal  elections in the coming six months seven  months is extremely important in  addition to that they need to realize  that if they leave  ministers who have already been  sanctioned by the u.s unsanctioned by  europe unsanctioned by other  international organizations those  ministers will keep on fighting even  violently then and their political  parties to stop the government from  moving forward or reforming thus putting  pressure on those individuals the  individuals responsible for the call of  protest with arms that led to the death  of six people and instigating fights  between christians and the shiite  community today the international  community is called upon  to accuse those individuals and take  sanctions the european parliament  previously a few months ago took a  decision on a framework and then voted  within the european parliament  on the entire framework for sanctions on  lebanese politicians for their  obstruction of justice or corruption now  is the time to start applying those  sanctions on those individuals because  if they don't put a limit and they cost  to their actions they're willing to get  us down to blood and war again to  maintain their power and to undermine  the state so the international community  needs to be extremely fair with those  political parties that have mobilized  militants with arms today and have taken  action they need to be sanctioned and  they need to be condemned  mark i'm really getting a sense of your  anger here  do you think that reflects the broader  lebanese community i mean is this going  to reignite the anger amongst the  lebanese  i was born in the 70s  and my parents used to hide me in  alleyways away from bombs and guns in  the lebanese civil war  today what we're mostly seeing and what  is really disturbing is we saw students  in those areas being  in put in hiding by their teachers from  bullets and bombs around them seeing  that is a terrifying experience another  generation being hid in alleyways and in  parkings and in basements again we don't  deserve this  they need to be stopped and we need to  find them now before this spreads and  hezbollah and their arms and the  militias again dominate a country for  their political power and not for the  governance or the benefit of the  lebanese people i think everyone in  those streets and neighborhoods that  were fighting today they know that they  are on a barrel about to explode they  need to step up they need to protect  their lives and the government and the  army need to be there for them until we  contain this and we blunt the attack and  the assault by hezbollah on the  judiciary this is the battle we need to  fight to avoid seeing our kids in  basements again  mark a lot of our audience is from  outside of lebanon can you get a sense  or give us a sense of what it's like to  live there now you know facing these  vast economic pressures uh the beirut  port explosion and the sense of death  that's happened over the last year or so  give us a sense of what it's like to  live in lebanon now and where you hope  the future lies  we are in a complete siege our money is  stuck in the banks  electricity is only provided for two  hours everything else is backup  generators  the costs of living have skyrocketed  because  the income and the currency has fallen  to bits and pieces  most people barely work  they just show up but there's nothing to  do  and now with the deterioration in the  security situation which already has  been through increase of thefts of  attacks of assaults and now  gunfights  this is we're besieged in our daily  lives in on every level economic social  and everything else people are sticking  to their homes doing the basics there is  no hope in the future they're just  hoping that tomorrow they'll be safe  that is how bad the situation is in  lebanon and then we've got the biggest  numbers of youth and capable individuals  emigrating  double-digit numbers of our healthcare  workers have left half businesses have  closed so  we're in very bad shape and yet  arms continue  to slaughter us in the streets because  they want to fight political battles  using the blood of innocent people in  the streets  returning to that conflict this morning  the interior minister says those who  prepared for the demonstration assured  us that it would be peaceful and the  shooting began with snipers taking aim  at people's heads what do you make of  his comments that they were assured that  it would be a peaceful demonstration  that would be an excellent line and a  stand-up comedy he knows hezbollah  except if he just came to the country  yesterday  i think his statement is a cover-up for  hezbollah and amal and he should be  condemned for that not only that he's an  active judge who asked to be part of  this government and conditioned that he  goes back to  the  judge role that he has this person is an  employee of the government who's  performing a role for  the current term just to have the title  i think it's more respectful for those  who died that he from the start is  responsible also for the protest and  what happens because his role as  minister of interior is to protect  people and he failed the least he could  have done after six people dying  being unable to protect homes and the  protest and everything if he had any  sense of decency as a judge or a human  he would have resigned and left home  instead he gave that funny ridiculous  statement which is a shame for a person  like him in that position to say after  people have died in the streets  how do you see the next hours and days  unfolding do you think that you are  likely to see that reform that you are  so desperately after  i think the fight now is to see if this  government can contain the onslaught the  second thing to watch out for is to see  judge bittar if he's going to continue  within his investigation  third is to see  movements and protest movements by  people especially the families of the  victims of whole those who died in the  august fourth explosion their actions  and how they're going to act because if  there are people in the streets if  there's a political fight and judicial  fight is on then yes the path of reform  is still open and we can move forward  and those are the three things i'll be  keeping an eye on speaking of one of  those things the investigation surely  now it's no longer  able to continue given what's happened  this morning and the pressure on the  judge how do you see it an internal  investigation unfolding surely it now  needs to go to perhaps an external party  to investigate  the process has already started within  the lebanese judiciary an external party  to investigate has to take the decision  by the government the government  currently is controlled by hezbollah the  prime minister is their ally i don't see  any international investigation coming  neither through a un security council  decision like the international tribunal  for the hariri assassination back in  2005 nor this current government with  michelle on as president miyati as prime  minister will do anything to upset  hezbollah that much they are his cronies  and i believe our best shot is still  protecting the lebanese investigation in  the short term if that fails we have no  recourse but to go for an international  investigation but that requires  international endorsement and i don't  think the countries such as france or  others  really have that as a priority for  themselves again the burden is on the  lebanese to fight the militias the  nonchalance of the international  community and an ability to protect the  judge and the legal system to withhold  the assault of hezbollah and their  weapons  well we appreciate your insights there  mark dao a professor of communications  at the american university of beirut  appreciate your time  thank you

